  "preview": "A glider drops fast at first, then catches a thermal and descends more gently. Two slopes, one flight. Which leg is steeper — and what does the y-intercept mean when there's no engine?",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Interpret rate of change (slope) in a piecewise, non-proportional context. Compare two slopes from different representations, recognize that a single relationship can have more than one rate, and connect the y-intercept to a real-world starting condition.",
    "studentPrompt": "A glider pilot releases from a tow plane at 2,400 meters of altitude. The flight has two distinct legs.\n\n**Leg 1 — the steep drop.** For the first 4 minutes after release, the glider descends at a steady rate. The pilot's altimeter records:\n\n| Time after release (min) | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Altitude (m) | 2400 | 2240 | 2080 | 1920 | 1760 |\n\n**Leg 2 — the thermal.** At 4 minutes, the glider catches a rising column of warm air. From that point, the pilot describes the descent this way:\n\n> \"After I caught the thermal, I lost altitude at only 40 meters per minute. I rode it like that for 12 more minutes before landing.\"\n\n1. What is the rate of descent during Leg 1? Write it as a labeled unit rate (with units) **and** as a slope (with a sign).\n2. Which leg is steeper? Explain what \"steeper\" means in this flight.\n3. At what altitude does the glider land?\n4. Sketch a graph of altitude vs. time for the whole flight. Label the slope of each leg on your graph.\n5. A second glider, released at the same time from the same altitude, descends at a constant 100 m/min the whole way down. Which glider lands first? Where are the two gliders at the moment the first one lands?",
    "image": {
      "kind": "url",
      "url": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/4534520/pexels-photo-4534520.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
      "alt": "From below of high metal Nivolet Cross with plane flying over cloudless blue sky in France",
      "credit": "Photo by Adrien Olichon on Pexels"
    },
    "launch": "Tell the story. \"A glider is an airplane with no engine — once it releases from the tow plane, gravity is pulling it down. The only thing that can keep it up is rising air called a thermal.\" Show the picture. Ask: \"What changes when the glider catches the thermal?\" Send groups to the boards.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Optional: graph paper for Q4",
      "Calculator allowed"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "rate-of-change",
      "slope",
      "piecewise-linear",
      "y-intercept",
      "real-world-application",
      "unit-rate"
    ],
    "taskType": "performance",
    "btcFit": "Strong BTC fit. Q1 is the low floor — anyone can compute a rate from the table. Q2 forces them to say what \"steeper\" means with signed numbers (since both slopes are negative). Q4 turns the whole flight into a piecewise graph — the diamond moment. Q5 is the high ceiling: simultaneous descents, where do they meet, who wins?",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: From the table, drop is 160 m each minute. Unit rate: 160 m/min descending. As a slope: m = -160 m/min (negative because altitude decreases).\n- Q2: Leg 1 (-160 m/min) is steeper than Leg 2 (-40 m/min). \"Steeper\" = larger absolute value of slope = altitude changing more per minute. Some groups will say Leg 2 is steeper because -40 > -160; the discussion should land on |slope|.\n- Q3: Altitude at start of Leg 2 = 2400 - 4(160) = 1760 m. Leg 2 drops for 12 min at 40 m/min = 480 m. Landing altitude = 1760 - 480 = 1280 m. Wait — that's still in the air! Strong groups will catch this and realize the pilot found a thermal good enough to keep them aloft above ground level for the whole 12 min, landing on a high plateau (or that this is the altitude when the 12 min ended, and the pilot landed there). Either reading is defensible.\n- Q4: Two line segments. Segment 1: from (0, 2400) to (4, 1760), slope -160. Segment 2: from (4, 1760) to (16, 1280), slope -40. Bent line — a \"piecewise\" graph.\n- Q5: Glider 2 at constant -100 m/min from 2400 m. It reaches 0 (ground) at 24 min. Glider 1 reaches 1280 m at 16 min (its \"landing\"). So glider 1 lands first if we accept 1280 as its landing altitude. At t = 16, glider 2 is at 2400 - 100(16) = 800 m. So when glider 1 \"lands\" at 1280 m, glider 2 is at 800 m — actually LOWER. That's the surprise.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Treating the whole flight as a single constant rate. (Averages 2400 - 1280 over 16 min = 70 m/min, which doesn't describe either leg accurately.)\n- Saying Leg 2 is steeper because -40 > -160 (confusing the value of the slope with its steepness).\n- Forgetting the sign on the slope. Calling 160 m/min the slope without the negative.\n- For Q5, comparing only the rates (-160 vs. -100) and concluding glider 1 must always be lower, without accounting for the thermal that flattens leg 2.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Both slopes are negative. What does the *sign* tell you? What does the *size* (absolute value) tell you?\n- Where is the y-intercept on your graph? What does it mean in the story? (2400 = altitude at release.)\n- For Q5, the glider with the *steeper* average descent ends up *higher* than the one with the constant descent. How is that possible?\n- If this glider's flight were modeled by a single equation y = mx + b, what would m and b be? Could it ever be done with one equation? Why or why not?",
    "extensions": "- Write a piecewise equation for the glider's altitude y as a function of time t. (y = 2400 - 160t for 0 ≤ t ≤ 4; y = 1760 - 40(t - 4) for 4 < t ≤ 16.)\n- A third glider's altitude is given only by these two points: (2, 2100) and (10, 1300). Is its descent rate constant? Find the rate. Compare to gliders 1 and 2.\n- Suppose the thermal in Leg 2 actually *lifted* the glider at +20 m/min instead. Redo Q3 and Q4. Could the glider land at all? When?\n- The pilot says, \"I lost 1,120 meters total during the whole flight.\" Use this to check or revise your answer to Q3.",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Leg 1: 160 m lost per minute. Unit rate: 160 m/min descending. Slope: m = -160 m/min.\n2. Leg 1 is steeper. \"Steeper\" means the absolute value of the slope is larger — altitude changes more per minute. |−160| > |−40|.\n3. End of Leg 1: 2400 - 4(160) = 1760 m. Leg 2 loses 12 × 40 = 480 m. Final altitude: 1760 - 480 = 1280 m (the glider is still 1,280 m up when the 12 min ends; in this story it lands on high terrain at that altitude, or the pilot keeps gliding from there).\n4. Two connected line segments forming a bent line: from (0, 2400) steeply down to (4, 1760), then more gently to (16, 1280). Slope labels: -160 m/min, -40 m/min.\n5. Glider 2: constant -100 m/min. At t = 16, altitude = 2400 - 1600 = 800 m. Glider 1 is at 1280 m — higher. The thermal saved it. Glider 2 reaches 0 at t = 24 min; glider 1 \"landed\" first at 1280 m at t = 16 min.",
